    "case_id": "amb1-case04-research-tool-release",
    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 2 USER]\ne011\tThat is close to what the maintainers say, but the structured preview has been “one more cohort” for six months.\ne012\tEach time we add users, we find another thing to document before going public.\ne013\tMeanwhile two volunteer contributors have stopped attending meetings because there is no visible release.\ne014\tI do not think contributor momentum is superficial.\ne015\tA tool that is always almost ready can become a private service for our friends.\n[Turn 2 ASSISTANT]\ne016\tAgreed—indefinite preview is itself a product choice, and it can narrow both the community and the evidence you receive.\ne017\tA public beta can create a focal point for contributors if its limits are explicit.\ne018\tThe maintainers' concern still matters because public access changes the volume and variety of support.\ne019\tThe question may be less “is it ready?”\ne020\tthan “what support promise accompanies the release, and can the team sustain that promise?”\n[Turn 3 USER]\ne021\tI am not sure disclaimers solve it.\ne022\tResearchers often ignore “beta” once they have invested in a workflow.\ne023\tIf we change the data model later, they may feel we broke something even if we warned them.\ne024\tAlso, the two labs want to cite the tool in papers this year.\ne025\tA public version would make that easier and may help our grant renewal, but I do not want citation pressure to freeze an interface we chose for convenience.\n[Turn 3 ASSISTANT]\ne026\tThen the most consequential commitment may be the data and output format, not the release label.\ne027\tYou could mark some interfaces as experimental while stabilizing a minimal exchange format for saved projects.\ne028\tBut that creates engineering work and may imply more compatibility than you can guarantee.\ne029\tThe publication and grant benefits are real organizational incentives; they should be named openly rather than translated into an argument that users necessarily need the current interface now.\n[Turn 4 USER]\ne030\tYou are assuming we can identify a minimal stable format.\ne031\tPriya, who designed the data layer, says that doing so before seeing stranger collections would formalize the wrong abstractions.\ne032\tShe wants public examples precisely to learn what should be stable.\ne033\tOur senior maintainer says that is using users to discover architecture at their expense.\ne034\tThey respect each other, but the conversation has become “learning” versus “responsibility,” and both words are doing too much work.\n[Turn 4 ASSISTANT]\ne035\tThose positions may differ on who bears the cost of uncertainty.\ne036\tPriya expects real use to reveal the right abstractions; the maintainer expects early users to absorb migration and support costs.\ne037\tA release design could make that cost visible and bounded—for example, limiting the beta to disposable projects or providing an export path—yet either choice may reduce the natural use you wanted to observe.\ne038\tThere may not be a way to learn from committed use without creating some obligation to those users.",
